    "body": "## Summary\n- add a real PostgreSQL/HTTP smoke for the signed Stripe `payment_intent.succeeded` transaction boundary\n- deliberately fail the matching wallet ledger insert after the PaymentIntent claim has been attempted\n- require the first signed webhook to return HTTP 500 and prove that the PaymentIntent claim, ledger row, wallet balance and funding lot all rolled back\n- remove the database failpoint and redeliver the exact same signed event; require exactly one successful payment claim, one wallet credit and one non-withdrawable `STRIPE_PAYMENT` funding lot\n- redeliver the event once more and prove duplicate handling cannot credit twice\n- document the transaction/crash contract for all Stripe webhook consumers and identify Stripe Connect transfer reversal as the only webhook path with an external provider write\n- no Flyway migration and no production-only test hook\n\n## Why this closes roadmap #733\nThe normal payment/refund/dispute webhook paths are local database/ledger transactions. Their safety depends on the provider-event claim not committing separately from the business/ledger settlement. This PR proves that boundary against the real PostgreSQL stack instead of relying only on mocks.\n\nThe payout failure webhook is the exceptional path that performs an external Stripe Transfer reversal. Its provider-success/local-rollback crash window was closed by #154 through authoritative Transfer re-read, reversal retry short-circuit and an unreversed-Transfer precondition before local `PAID`.\n\n## Validation required before merge\n- exact-head Maven/API verify\n- Web dependency audit/test/lint/build\n- Production Compose contract\n- full Containers/runtime smoke including the new signed-webhook forced rollback/retry scenario\n- unchanged latest master and mergeable exact head verification\n\nMerge only with a merge commit after every exact-head check is completed and green.",
